----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** "The Call of Cthulhu" by H. P. Lovecraft



//Results:// An academic named Professor Fredericks is mentioned in the Archaeological Society meeting. Afterwards, SCP-423 stated the story was "dense," but still with lots of room to move around.



----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** Printed image of Byblos Syllabary Document d



//Results:// Seven characters changed, near the middle of the third line.  Using the 'reversed k' symbol added as a sign for the middle of SCP-423's name, the sign resembling a tic-tac-toe board may be guessed to correspond to the letter F. Curiously, as the signs following the 'reversed k' are unchanged, despite what would be expected for a Semitic language, the 'Dalet' symbol was not inserted. SCP-423 referred to the text, afterwards, as "boring". 



----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** //The 120 Days of Sodom, or the School of Libertinage// by Donatien Alphonse François, Marquis de Sade (translated from French)



//Results:// A fifth 'master' appears in the book, named Frederick and no last name given. This character does not partake in any of the torture and rape, instead spending most of his time talking to the four madams. The other masters express great mistrust regarding this person due to the apparent discomfort caused to him by all the torment inflicted upon members of the masters' harem. Halfway through the book, this character flees the castle, taking several members of the harem with himself and leaving behind a note expressing disapproval regarding the work of the other masters. The remaining four masters later become obsessed with the escapee, fearing that he might alert the German police force to have them arrested. The book ends with the four masters committing mass suicide shortly after releasing all their hostages into the forests.



SCP-423 commented on this experience by stating "I tried giving it a chance because it's considered a classic, but halfway through, I realized I just can't take any of this."



//Notes:// What the [EXPLETIVE REDACTED]? Why'd you do this to the poor guy? I thought we agreed on introducing SCP-423 to erotic works no more? - Dr. Vang



Well... Honestly... I thought it would be funny. - Dr. Chair



Go [EXPLETIVE REDACTED] yourself Alan. - Dr. Edison



----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** [[[SCP-152]]]



//Results:// A character with the name of "Fred" or a variation thereof appeared in several of the stories found in SCP-152. All stories exhibiting SCP-423 carried a common aspect: Each of the stories in which the aforementioned character appears include a radical transformation of the story's setting into a post-apocalyptic wasteland inhabited by a sparse number of humans. The character appears as a survivor of the apocalypse and usually as part of a larger gang of survivors wandering the wasteland in search of other humans and nutrition. SCP-423 displayed the ability to alter the narrative of the stories within SCP-152 to its own liking, similar to how SCP-152's writing style alters itself when exposed to a human reader.



//Notes:// After returning to its journal, SCP-423 expressed that it had found the experience to be both extremely exciting and adventurous. SCP-423 requested to be allowed access to SCP-152 again in future experiments. Request to periodically expose SCP-423 to SCP-152 as a reward for good behavior is pending.



----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** //The Shadow of the Torturer//, by Gene Wolfe



//Results:// SCP-423 disappeared for several hours; however, no notable changes occurred to the text. Upon returning, it took SCP-423 two hours to recuperate. Upon questioning, SCP-423 related having briefly interacted with the character of Severian but was equally confused that the encounter wasn't recorded in the text. SCP-423 expressed reluctance to reenter, stating, "It's denser than gravity." 



//Notes:// //I have a theory. I think because of the unreliable nature of Severian's narration, which includes leaving out certain events, SCP-423's encounter also wasn't recorded. How or why that's possible, I'm not sure. In any case, I'm interested in more texts like this one to experiment with. I'll try another Gene Wolfe story later.// - Researcher Lang



----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** Text regarding **[[span class="taboo"]][[[https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/taboo|the province devoid of labels]]][[/span]]**.



//Results:// Testing forbidden by O5-█.



----



**Date:** ██/██/████



**Test Material:** "Ender's Game", by Orson Scott Card. 



Results: Throughout the narrative, a newly enrolled student named Fred consistently emerges, making multiple appearances across various sections of the story. Notably, Fred is prominently featured in numerous battle room games, swiftly challenging opponents in succession, ultimately earning a place within Ender's Dragon Army.



----



**Date:** ██/██/████



**Test Material:** [https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/experiment-log-t-98816-oc108-682 Experiment Log T-98816-OC108/682]



Results: An entry surfaced within the Experiment Logs detailing an experiment conducted by "Guest Researcher Fred." The experiment proposed a potential crossover scenario between SCP-████ and [[[SCP-682]]].



Notes: //You know, it's actually quite interesting. We never really thought about testing SCP-████ with SCP-682 before. Maybe we should give it a try sometime.// -Dr. Avenlee



//Proposal denied.// -O5-█



----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** "Moon Rising" (//Wings of Fire// book 6), by Tui T. Sutherland



//Results:// Instances of the normally one-off SeaWing Barracuda were replaced by one named Ford. Ford took a larger role in the story than Barracuda (though not as large as the characters in the primary Jade Winglet): he was explicitly named in the opening, where it was also found that his mind was unreadable by Moonwatcher; he was present in the underground lake Turtle and Moon went to after the history cave explosion, where he was noticeably concerned about the prospect of a mind reader before Moon told him that his mind was also unreadable; he fled from the cafeteria with everyone else present when the stalactite nearly killed Icicle; and when Turtle told Moon about how he got his skyfire[[footnote]] A stone that prevents the wearer's mind from being read [[/footnote]], he noted that Ford had come with him and took one piece for himself, leaving Turtle the other five. He was described by Turtle as an old commoner friend of his, with a bad habit of snooping around in the Summer Palace when he wasn't supposed to. No character took this role in the original story.



When SCP-423 returned to the journal, it noted that the book "had a weird vibe, like something bad was underneath me all the time, and it could see into my head."



//Notes: His aversion to death also applies to having his mind read, it seems. Wonder what secrets he's hiding.// - Junior Researcher Olivia



----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** Hardcover copy of ”The Outsider” by Stephen King.



//Results:// Notable alterations were made to the ending of the novel, including the appearance of a character named ”Officer S. C. Frederickson”, who attempted to dissuade the main characters from entering the location of the climax of the novel (a cave system).



The detonation of several explosives around the cave entrances was initiated by ”Officer Frederickson”, upon the characters ignoring their requests.



Events played out as normal from the point after the encounter with the titular ”Outsider” character. No alterations were made to the rest of the novel.



//Notes:// Upon questioning, SCP-423 has stated that //”the ending sucked”// (sic), and did not elaborate any further. - Dr. [[[Just Fragments Hub|Adam Krug]]]



-----

**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** "The Ingenious Gentleman Don Quixote of La Mancha", by Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra (translated from Spanish)

 

//Results:// A character named Federico appears in multiple scenes amongst the crowd reacting to Don Quixote's actions. In his third appearance, the character of Don Quixote confronts him directly, asking him why he keeps following them. Federico claims that he is just an onlooker interested in his adventures. Don Quixote doesn't believe him and claims he must be an agent from "The Wise Freston." At this point, Don Quixote tries to attack Federico. Federico manages to escape, and it's not seen nor mentioned for the rest of the book.

 

//Notes:// After returning to its diary, SCP-423 was asked about the incident. It claimed "[[[ SCP-4028| that guy was completely insane.]]]" No other explanation was given. - Junior Researcher Pupa



-----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** //The Path of Daggers// by Robert Jordan. SCP-423 was requested beforehand to take on the role of a male channeler. 



//Results:// SCP-423 appeared as an Asha'man named Fred, suffering from various mental disorders due to his connection to the One Power. Fred's connection to the One Power drove him irretrievably insane towards the end of the book, though other characters were unable to gentle or kill him before he escaped via Traveling to the Land of Madmen.[[footnote]]This location was never visited by any characters in any of the books in this series.[[/footnote]]



//Notes:// Upon returning to its journal, SCP-423 showed none of the symptoms displayed during its time in this book, though it has stated a wish to avoid taking any roles involving insane characters in the future.



-----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** //Empress Theresa// by Norman Boutin.



//Results:// SCP-423 appears briefly as a reporter named Fred who has no lines. Upon seeing people praise the titular character he proceeds to walk away, but Theresa notices. She then asks why SCP-423 is 'unimpressed with the Savior of Mankind'. SCP-423 gabbles something about how he needed the bathroom, upon which Theresa lets him. SCP-423 does not come back after this.



When SCP-423 returned to the diary, it claimed that the book's narrative drivel started to give him a headache. Upon being asked about the character of Theresa Sullivan-Hartley herself, SCP-423 was silent for a moment, before answering, "It was like staring into a dark, empty void like ice goes down your spine. I don't like that she was aware of me. I think so, anyway."



-----



**Date: ██/██/████**

**Test Material:** "Black Rose", by Sévérine Paulo (Translated from French) 



//Results:// SCP-423 manifested as a minor character as part of the group of refugees the protagonist is protecting. Its only notable appearance is when the character distracts the seventh general of the "Black Thorn Kingdom" allowing the protagonist to defeat him. 



//Notes:// Despite the existence of this document, no Foundation personnel has [[[SCP-2747|any memory]]] of this test or the book "Black Rose". On top of this, aside from this document, there's no other record of the existence of the book "Black Rose". When questioned about this, SCP-423 claimed to remember the test but refused to give any further explanation, stating, "I really don't want to piss off the [[[https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/why-is-five-afraid-of-seven|Queen]]] by telling you." 



-----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** "The Eye of Argon" by Jim Theis



//Results:// SCP-423 manifested as a mercenary named Fredrichson, hired by a priest to kill Grignr. After failing to do so, SCP-423 immediately returned to its journal.



//Notes:// No other changes were seen in the story. SCP-423 wrote this after returning:



> I-I have no words on what was this supposed to be. I hear this was pretty laughable at the time. I wonder what happened to the author?



-----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** A tattoo in the left forearm of D-3279, which reads //I will always love you Cindy//



//Results:// Tattoo changed to //I and Fred will always love you Cindy//. Each letter was now slightly smaller to compensate for the limited amount of ink. 



//Notes:// D-3279 claimed not to feel any pain or distress during the process. 



-----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** A printout of the gamescript for "Super Mario 64". Result was laser-scanned and the raw text compiled into a working program after being screened for anomalies.



//Results:// The models for the characters of the "Lakitu Bros." were changed to possess blue eyes. All instances of their name in dialogue were changed to Fred. 



-----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** The Best Gorilla Joke of 1897



//Results:// Gorilla: Did you hear about the gorilla who escaped from the zoo?



Zookeeper: No, I did not.



Gorilla: That is because I am a quiet gorilla.



[Muffled sounds of gorilla violence as Fred slowly walks away]



//Notes:// They're right, it was funny. - Junior Researcher Pupa 



-----



**Date:** ██/██/████

**Test Material:** A copy of the SCP-423 altered version of "Tom Sawyer", by Mark Twain



//Results:// No change. 



//Notes:// After SCP-423 returned to the diary, it stated, "It felt weird, like deja vu. I didn't feel any resistance when getting in, but at the same time I couldn't do anything different. I really didn't like that feeling." Possibility of use as punishment pending.  





